# A native test binary that dies mid-suite, and the report a reader gets.
#
# **A block that passed may never be reported as failing.** A failed assertion
# is an abort and the process names the block it was in on the way out (SPEC
# 6.9, `cli/runtime/testing.rs`). A process that runs out of stack names no
# block: it says why on stderr and exits from its fault handler. The runner
# used to blame the block it had asked that process to start at, so one block
# walking off the end of the Buri stack cost every block from there on its
# verdict.
#
# What makes the attribution a fact rather than a guess is that every block
# writes a line when it returns, so the block a dead process was in is the first
# one that did not. Two of the three here pass and are reported as passing; the
# one that died is named, with what the process said.
#
# The negative twin is the base case: with it the recursion returns, and the
# same three blocks all pass.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "a block that ends the test process is the block the report names, and the blocks around it keep their verdicts"

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/deep", "--force"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "died.txt"
}

# The negative twin: a base case, and the same three blocks pass.
edit {
  file: "lib/deep/deep.buri"
  replace: "if (n == 0) { 0 } else { deep(n + 1) + 1 }"
  with: "if (n == 0) { 0 } else { deep(n - 1) + 1 }"
}

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/deep", "--force"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "clean.txt"
}
